{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q006_ubiquitin_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe groups ubiquitin-related proteins, UBX domains, and Ras-binding domains into distinct families within the ubiquitin-like superfamily (d.15.1), in the ubiquitin-like fold (d.15) and alpha+beta class (d). Their superfamily relationship supports probable remote common ancestry and diversification of a shared beta-grasp scaffold; sharing a fold alone would be weaker evidence for homology.

The useful biological connection is how this scaffold supports different molecular interactions. Ubiquitin and many ubiquitin-related modifiers participate in covalent protein modification. UBX domains typically mediate noncovalent interactions with the p97/VCP/Cdc48 ATPase in adaptor proteins. Ras-binding domains connect activated Ras-family GTPases to effectors; they are not themselves Ras-like GTPases.

It would be unsafe to annotate every member as ubiquitin, a conjugatable modifier, or a proteasomal degradation signal. Likewise, p97 binding cannot be transferred from UBX domains to all superfamily members, nor Ras binding from Ras-binding domains to ubiquitin-related proteins. Even ubiquitin modification does not invariably signal degradation. The shared scaffold can guide structural comparison or core-template selection, but does not establish interchangeable binding surfaces or functions. Specific annotations require sequence and domain-context evidence, relevant terminal or interface features, and biochemical binding or conjugation evidence; complex structures can establish interface compatibility.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
